1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:05,549
Secure GPU Stack, version 0.1.1.

2
00:00:05,669 --> 00:00:13,418
Since the last video, the stack has run on a modern GPU. Here's what that showed, what it found, and what's next.

3
00:00:15,118 --> 00:00:22,624
The last video ended on a limit: krabs's GPU is from 2016, too old for current PyTorch and JAX.

4
00:00:22,744 --> 00:00:31,644
So the next machine was M2, a desktop with an RTX 3080. That's Ampere, the generation of Lambda's A10 and A100.

5
00:00:31,764 --> 00:00:39,808
M2 doesn't run Ubuntu, so the card goes to an Ubuntu VM instead. That's also how a Lambda instance gets its GPU.

6
00:00:41,508 --> 00:00:50,390
VM B booted the way a Lambda customer's machine would: a fresh Ubuntu image, our cloud-init file, and the published release, with its hash pinned.

7
00:00:50,510 --> 00:00:56,636
Eight minutes later, unattended, it had rebooted with NVIDIA's open driver and kernel lockdown on.

8
00:00:56,756 --> 00:01:02,879
That's the driver path every current Lambda GPU takes, and the old laptop couldn't test it.

9
00:01:04,579 --> 00:01:08,221
And it found five problems the old laptop never could.

10
00:01:08,341 --> 00:01:18,791
The big one: every TensorFlow convolution failed. TensorFlow's cuDNN pulled in parts of the system's other cuDNN: same version number, built for another CUDA.

11
00:01:18,911 --> 00:01:22,421
Matrix multiplies still worked, so the check passed it.

12
00:01:22,541 --> 00:01:27,674
Ubuntu's crash reporter quietly undid one of our kernel settings at every reboot.

13
00:01:27,794 --> 00:01:38,832
The check's GPU test only multiplied matrices. TensorFlow's compiler couldn't find NVIDIA's assembler without a CUDA toolkit. And the check raised a false alarm for every new Jupyter user.

14
00:01:38,952 --> 00:01:45,192
All five are fixed, verified in the VM, and released as version 0.1.1.

15
00:01:47,092 --> 00:01:51,886
Then VM A, for Lambda Stack, with Lambda's own install script.

16
00:01:52,006 --> 00:02:02,619
It failed. Neither driver that Lambda's repository pins compiles on Ubuntu's current kernel, so a fresh Ubuntu 24.04 machine ends up with no GPU driver.

17
00:02:02,739 --> 00:02:06,214
That's new finding fifteen, rated high.

18
00:02:06,334 --> 00:02:14,876
To still compare, VM A got the same Ubuntu driver as ours, then Lambda's frameworks, installed exactly as its script does.

19
00:02:16,776 --> 00:02:20,168
Same GPU, same driver, same check.

20
00:02:20,288 --> 00:02:29,049
Lambda Stack: nineteen pass, and seventeen warnings of its own. Secure GPU Stack: fifty-six pass, zero warnings.

21
00:02:29,169 --> 00:02:38,738
On Lambda Stack, any account on the machine could use the GPU. On ours, another account tried 1.2 million times and got nowhere.

22
00:02:38,858 --> 00:02:46,548
And speed: ResNet-50 trained at 902 images a second on Lambda Stack, and 919 on ours.

23
00:02:46,668 --> 00:02:52,084
Bare metal managed 904, so the VM itself costs nothing measurable.

24
00:02:54,184 --> 00:02:55,617
What's next.

25
00:02:55,737 --> 00:03:02,182
Lambda's own cloud image: whether its JupyterLab is exposed, its default users, and Secure Boot.

26
00:03:02,302 --> 00:03:07,543
Data-center GPUs and multi-GPU NCCL, on a real Lambda instance.

27
00:03:07,663 --> 00:03:12,469
Whether existing Lambda Stack machines lose their driver at the next kernel update.

28
00:03:12,589 --> 00:03:17,995
And a release signing key, so downloads are checked by signature, not just by hash.

29
00:03:24,795 --> 00:03:30,783
Secure GPU Stack 0.1.1: tested on a modern GPU, and out now.

30
00:03:31,083 --> 00:03:34,443
Next stop: a real Lambda instance.
